{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (stable release 2.08): Actin/HSP70 is family c.55.1.1 (sunid 53068). Hexokinase is family c.55.1.3 (sunid 53083; protein-level Hexokinase 53084). Glycerol kinase is family c.55.1.4 (sunid 53089; protein-level Glycerol kinase 53090). All three lineages run root > class c (alpha and beta proteins) > fold c.55 (Ribonuclease H-like motif) > superfamily c.55.1 (Actin-like ATPase domain, sunid 53067) > family. The superfamily record has homology_scope 'sf', no warnings or heterogeneity, and the comment 'duplication contains two domains of this fold'. The family records 53068, 53083 and 53089 each report homology_implied_by_level true with no warnings.

Interpretation: SCOPe places the three families in different families but the same superfamily, c.55.1. A shared superfamily is SCOPe's homology-bearing level for probable common ancestry, so SCOPe asserts remote common ancestry of the actin-like ATPase domain architecture across these three families. It makes no closer (family-level) homology claim between them. This permits the inference that their catalytic domains share an ancestor at superfamily depth. It does not permit inferring a shared substrate, reaction, cellular role, or interchangeable structural template; the SCOPe records here state none of those. Their divergent roles (actin/HSP70 in cytoskeleton and chaperone functions, hexokinase in hexose phosphorylation, glycerol kinase in glycerol phosphorylation) are consistent with divergence after a common ancestor, but that is biological interpretation, not a SCOPe assertion. Any specific claim about shared ATP-binding chemistry needs an external source such as an alignment, a structure, or a paper, none of which was retrieved here.

Limits: the claim rests only on classification placement in release 2.08 and makes no sequence, BLAST, FATCAT, or residue-mapping claim. No historical or periodic-update (2.08-2026-08-16) claim is made. The search for the literal phrase 'glycerol kinase' returned no hits; the glycerol kinase node was found by searching 'glycerol' and then verified by exact sunid lookup. The answer does not state that these domains are unrelated or convergent; SCOPe's superfamily placement is simply the positive homology assertion it makes.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=actin&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=hexokinase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol+kinase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1.3/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1.4/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53068/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53083/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53089","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53089/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53089/annotations"]}]}
